WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.730
- I better watch out I don't wanna step on

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:02.563
one of these little
- [Anna] I know, I know.

00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:04.006
- doggies right here.

00:00:04.006 --> 00:00:04.839
- (laughing) I love it!

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:06.210
Well, welcome! Have you ever met Anna?

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.466
- Well, briefly backstage, but we're

00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:09.299
old friends now.
- Yeah.

00:00:09.299 --> 00:00:10.656
- But before today, no?

00:00:10.656 --> 00:00:11.514
- No.

00:00:11.514 --> 00:00:12.347
- I just like bringing people together.

00:00:12.347 --> 00:00:13.180
- I feel like we're on

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.710
opposing teams though, which--

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.170
- Well, we are actually.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.913
- Someone took my pom poms away.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.636
- Look at all these dogs, this is amazing!

00:00:20.636 --> 00:00:21.469
- I know, it's--

00:00:21.469 --> 00:00:22.380
- I saw one pooped earlier,

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.100
I was scared about that.
- Right in front of my chair.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.053
It was awesome.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.000
So you're working the big game, right?

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.300
- Yeah.
- So who are you rooting for?

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.460
- Hey, guy. I'm rooting for the

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.155
Kansas City Chiefs, come on.

00:00:34.155 --> 00:00:36.400
(speaking in Spanish) The Chiefs!

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.720
- I mean, I like the initials, so I'm in.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:40.580
It's a great initial.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.320
So I'm not usually one
to be all up in sports

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.030
world but I love when it's challenging

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.120
and it's neck and neck,
it makes it more fun.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.870
- It's always more fun
when it's competitive.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:49.970
- Tony, can I ask you something?

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:50.803
- Sure.

00:00:50.803 --> 00:00:53.430
- So as somebody who
doesn't know when they're

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.060
watching a football game, what they're

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:56.854
actually watching--

00:00:56.854 --> 00:00:57.687
(audience laughing)

00:00:57.687 --> 00:00:59.160
- You know other people not us.

00:01:00.243 --> 00:01:02.360
- But what should I be looking for

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.510
on the big game day?

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.284
I think you're about to be mounted.

00:01:05.284 --> 00:01:07.458
- I think he's humping my foot.

00:01:07.458 --> 00:01:08.990
He's a little too young
for that, but it's good.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.770
- Tony, what do I look
for? Who do I look for?

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.570
Do I look for an offensive strategy?

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.083
Do I look for defensive? What am I,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.100
give me some pointers.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.289
Do I just go for food?

00:01:20.289 --> 00:01:21.774
(laughing)

00:01:21.774 --> 00:01:22.607
- Food is--

00:01:22.607 --> 00:01:23.720
- What's something clever we can say

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:24.962
when people are watching?
- Food is always a good

00:01:24.962 --> 00:01:25.795
way to go.

00:01:25.795 --> 00:01:27.157
- What's something clever? Good question.

00:01:27.157 --> 00:01:28.316
- Just make us sound good.

00:01:28.316 --> 00:01:29.900
- Okay, do you guys know what an RPO is?

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:30.920
- Totally.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.385
- You know what an RPO is?

00:01:32.385 --> 00:01:35.245
- A real player, oh yeah!

00:01:35.245 --> 00:01:36.400
(laughing)

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.803
- Real players only!

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:39.553
- Yeah, that was better.

00:01:39.553 --> 00:01:41.670
- RPO is run-pass
option. So it's something

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.330
we say a lot in our business.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.530
You'll say, "Look at the RPO here."

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.180
That means when the
quarterback gets the ball

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.230
he can either have the
run where he does this,

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.970
and he can pull it away, then he can pass,

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.150
or he can take off and run it.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.820
So we call that an RPO.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.010
So that makes you sound smart.

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.740
Go, "They need more RPOs right now."

00:01:58.740 --> 00:01:59.861
To get the ball going.

00:01:59.861 --> 00:02:02.834
- It sounds Star Trek.
Or like Star Wars. R2-D2.

00:02:02.834 --> 00:02:03.667
- [Anna] Can you also tell me

00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:04.550
what a tight end does?

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:05.420
- What a tight end does?

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.269
- Yes, I know this is embarrassing.

00:02:07.269 --> 00:02:08.563
- Want me to show you
my tight end right here?

00:02:08.563 --> 00:02:09.659
(laughing and cheering)

00:02:09.659 --> 00:02:12.159
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, all right.

00:02:14.128 --> 00:02:15.840
- Just so you know what
the difference looks like

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.845
this is what a non-tight end looks like.

00:02:17.845 --> 00:02:20.133
(laughing)

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:21.710
Just so you have some comparison.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:23.390
I'm just saying.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:24.800
This is actually serious
though, you just made

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.686
the Hall of Fame and--

00:02:26.686 --> 00:02:28.391
(cheering)

00:02:28.391 --> 00:02:29.224
- Thank you. Thank you.

00:02:29.224 --> 00:02:30.553
- And I think that is amazing.

00:02:30.553 --> 00:02:32.540
That is so amazing. But even cooler

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.640
you got Father of the Year!

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.224
Are your kids proud?

00:02:36.224 --> 00:02:37.057
- (chuckles) Are my kids proud?

00:02:37.057 --> 00:02:39.431
They were asking me, how the hell did you

00:02:39.431 --> 00:02:41.283
get that award? They were like,

00:02:41.283 --> 00:02:43.487
"Who voted for this? I didn't get a vote."

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.550
But it was good, it was good.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.260
Honestly, one of the biggest honors

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.580
I've ever had in my life.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.580
They flew us out to New York.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:52.940
We did a whole presentation.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.580
The people that have
received Father of the Year,

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.330
it goes back 60 years,
presidents of our country

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:00.506
have received this award
so it was very cool.

00:03:00.506 --> 00:03:01.339
Very cool.

00:03:01.339 --> 00:03:02.935
- Oh my gosh, how
prestigious. That's amazing!

00:03:02.935 --> 00:03:04.318
(applause)

00:03:04.318 --> 00:03:05.790
That's really cool.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.680
And you have a new podcast right?

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.183
- Yeah. I've got a
podcast called Wide Open.

00:03:10.183 --> 00:03:11.357
- I get it.

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.590
- Get it? Play on words.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.700
But it's where I bring on a guest,

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.020
people like yourselves,
movers and shakers,

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:21.260
people from leaders of industry, actors,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.920
athletes, doctors, and we talk about

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.203
leveling up, being the
best version of yourself.

00:03:26.203 --> 00:03:28.840
'Cause success leaves
clues and I love to get

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.897
people's backstory and say,

00:03:30.897 --> 00:03:33.720
"How do you respond to the critiques?

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.650
How do you get out of a depression?

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.690
How do you just get better?"

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:39.687
And then have some fun with it.

00:03:39.687 --> 00:03:40.890
- How do you deal with it all?

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.000
Yeah, that's a great podcast.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.740
- Also what is your workout routine?

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:44.832
I want to know how you think, I want to

00:03:44.832 --> 00:03:45.875
get deep inside your brain.

00:03:45.875 --> 00:03:47.120
- Oh, it's easy! I just
pick my children up,

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.288
and then set them down, and that's it.

00:03:49.288 --> 00:03:50.970
(laughing)

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.480
So I asked Anna this too

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.800
but who's your dream podcast person?

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.650
And if you say Lil' Kim it's awesome.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.010
- Is that your dream
cast? Has she given out

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:01.523
good relationship advice?

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.463
- I love Barack Obama.

00:04:06.614 --> 00:04:07.940
(applause)

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.613
- Fine, Tony!

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.100
- And then somebody else I've always loved

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.416
from afar is Meryl Streep.

00:04:16.416 --> 00:04:17.690
I think she would be amazing.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.970
- I would love to have Winston Churchill

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.010
dig himself out from the grave!

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:23.610
- Officially, Tony Gonzalez is my favorite

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:25.470
person to be on this show

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:28.510
because that is amazing! You know why?

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.570
I agree too though. She's
got four kids as well

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.420
and just such a fascinating career.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:33.890
I would listen to that.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:35.560
I'd want to know why, what makes her tick,

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:36.830
and what made her really push herself.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:37.663
That's a good one.

00:04:37.663 --> 00:04:40.060
- Well, I've seen her
do an interview before.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.080
- Me too. I had several. I'm a stalker.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:42.913
- Are you a stalker?

00:04:42.913 --> 00:04:44.110
- Totally!

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.950
- So she was doing her interview and

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.640
they asked her like, "What is your secret?

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.020
How are you such a great actress?

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.770
How do you get into the moment so well?"

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.950
And she was like, "I don't know."

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.017
She literally said, "I don't know."

00:04:55.017 --> 00:04:58.050
And she said, "I can't teach this."

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:58.883
- She's a do-er. Yeah.

00:04:58.883 --> 00:05:00.450
- And I would love to be
able to go on the show

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.030
and try to draw that out of her,

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:03.060
try to see exactly,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:03.893
because there is something there.

00:05:03.893 --> 00:05:05.680
- Because now it's innate but she probably

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.310
had to work, what
happened when you started

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:09.480
though would be the question.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.177
- Well, Tony don't you think that,

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:13.427
do you get into that
mentality when you play?

00:05:15.735 --> 00:05:17.495
- You get so focused?
- There's elements

00:05:17.495 --> 00:05:19.400
of your brain and maybe when you perform,

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.210
there's elements of your
brain that sort of shut off

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:24.870
and you focus, I don't know.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.020
- Well, you do know because
you're a great actress

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:28.470
and so you've done it before.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.580
No, you've done it before.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.700
And for anybody out there watching,

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.020
there are techniques
to get into that flow,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.020
that state of where you
can block out everything

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:40.290
and you're just absolutely present and

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.590
it's kind of like not
being afraid anymore.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:45.060
How do you get beyond that fear?

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:45.893
I always say

00:05:45.893 --> 00:05:47.820
life takes off on the other side of fear

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:49.450
and when you can get to that point

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:50.600
there's nothing you can't accomplish.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.240
And I would love to see what Meryl

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:53.312
has to say about that.

00:05:53.312 --> 00:05:54.470
- I'm only 37 and know nothing but

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.730
I do feel like ego has
a lot to do with it.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.420
I feel like you have to let go of your ego

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.420
and importance on, you
know what I'm saying?

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:03.670
- You know what I say about ego?

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:06.030
I say ego has no amigo.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.926
- No! Wow, that's good!

00:06:07.926 --> 00:06:08.759
- That's a good one, huh?

00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:09.920
- Stitch that on a pillow!

